From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wu Yuwei <yuweiwu@pm.me>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:07:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Edges of non-atomic inline boxes

`InlineBoxKind::StartEdge` and `EndEdge`: the start and end margin, border
and padding of a CSS `display: inline` element. An edge sits at a character
boundary like an out-of-flow box, so the text on its two sides is shaped and
analyzed as if it weren't there, but it takes up its width on the line. It
adds nothing to the line's height.

An edge has no soft wrap opportunity of its own. A start edge stays with the
content after it (that content's opportunity is taken before the edge, once
for consecutive edges), an end edge with the content before it. One that
does not fit takes the last opportunity on the line, as text does, or
overflows. Whitespace before an edge is not at the end of the line, so it
does not hang.
